Date: Sun, 21 Feb 93 08:58:40 PST
From: hal@alumni.cco.caltech.edu (Hal Finney)
Subject: THEORY: Information Theory and Extropians

Tim writes about Cover's book on information theory.  I hope I am
able to find it soon.

I am especially interested in the application of his ideas to investment
strategy.  I am no "trader"; buy-and-hold is more my style.  I have
often used dollar-cost averaging, where I invest gradually over a period
of time in order to keep the average cost lower.  Cover's ideas seem
to be an extension of this strategy so I find them very appealing.

One thing that surprises me is that these ideas don't seem to have made
their way into the popular financial media.  I used to read various
magazines on this subject, and they were full of the latest "hot" high-tech
ideas for making money: chaos theory, neural networks, etc.  Lots
of these were not very practical, IMO.  But I never read about Cover's
model for creating portfolios which did "asymptotically" as well as the
best-performing investment in the portfolio.  Maybe the "asymptotic" part
is too long-term for the people reading these magazines.

Hal

