Date: Sun, 21 Feb 1993 12:13:59 -0500
From: deloura@cs.unc.edu (Mark A. DeLoura)
Subject: EMAIL ADS

While I can certainly see the usefulness in Michael Ney's vision
of being able to order products and services via email, I think
that at this point the risks outweigh the advantages.

Already I receive mail from several mailing lists I never requested
addition to, and unfortunately there is no standard header line,
or subject prefix, that will identify a mass-junk-mailing.

Technologically speaking, sending ads over email would place an additional
burden on the net itself.  10Kbyte ad * 10000 recipients = many bytes.

Furthermore, most of those ads will probably go to /dev/null anyway.

Unfortunately, advertisers have us in their grip now-- if they send us
email, they know that we will have to at least read the first page to
determine what it is.  It would be nice to not have to read ads if
we choose not to.  (I can always trash physical mail as soon as I see 
the words "Urgent Reply Requested!" or Ed McMahon's picture.)

A standard header "Mass-mailing: advertising" on email junk would
be helpful, though that would probably be analogous to advertisers
putting an electronic signal between commercials to let smart VCRs
cancel out recording of the commercials-- think this will ever happen?

What I want is an intelligent agent, that can determine content of 
my mail and let me know what is junk, so that I don't have to dig
through hundreds of Ks of useless dreck.

Another problem I have with advertising over the net is the ease of
forging mail-- I could sign everyone I know up for a whole bunch of 
mailing lists (hey, wait-- I can do that *now!*), or send in purchase
orders for people.  All I would need is a credit card number, and 
that's fairly easy to get ahold of, as well.

I figure that someday, pretty soon actually, Publisher's Clearing House
will get on the net.  Then we'll *all* be in trouble. :)
	---Mark

