Date: Sun, 21 Feb 93 20:16:51 GMT
From: "Stephen J. Whitrow" <sjw@liberty.demon.co.uk>
Subject: ECON: Cover's ideas

Hal Finney writes:

> Robin and Tim have both posted about Thomas M. Cover's writings on
> economics and finance.    [....]

> One paper had some results which I found amazingly paradoxical.  Suppose
> there is an investment, say a stock, which in each period will either
> double in value or lose half its value.  If these happened with 50-50
> likelihood, then an investment in this stock would have no net gain over
> a period of time.  This points out the importance of looking at "log
> return"; a factor of 2 and a factor of 1/2 are equal and opposite in
> log terms.

I think linear return is still important, though. It would be worth
diversifying in a large portfolio of these stocks. It doesn't matter how many
'periods' you hold the investments for. The more, the better.

Each individual investment per period involves receiving odds of 2 / 1 against
about a true even money chance. This yields an average return of 50% of
turnover, taking the 'bet' as being the maximum loss per period per
investment (a 1/2 point stake). Or 25% on total investment.


            R = (Pm + 1) x W%  - 100

where:   
      
       R   =  return on total turnover staked (as a percentage)
       Pm  =  mean price obtained about each winning selection 
       W%  =  winning percentage

This formula would tell us whether a profit or loss had been obtained for a
given gambling situation. Next you could be more conservative in calculating
the real return, allowing that the total bank includes a reserve that is not
normally staked, and maybe allowing for inflation, or even comparing with the
average gain of all stocks over the period.  

Over a number of periods some would have gone up by 8, 16 or 32 times, some 
would still be their original value, whilst the losers would not even have
been total losses, and number of winners = number of losers. Provided the
length of one 'period' is not several years this would be an excellent
investment. Unfortunately, I hardly think methods for locating these stocks
would be broadcast to all and sundry for nowt, by those in the know!

