Date: Sun, 21 Feb 1993 20:43:59 -0800 (PST)
From: Scott C DeLancey <delancey@darkwing.uoregon.edu>
Subject: LAW: "initiating force"

> I salute Scott for responding to my challenge that he append citations in
> support of his claims about property rights among animals and in primitive
> human societies.  I was hoping he would quote some of these sources, much
> as I quoted Hayek.  I guess that is asking a lot, though.  

In fact it is, as far as the ethological literature is concerned.  If 
were to post an assertion that pigs can't fly, and I were to respond
with a demand for references, how much time would you spend looking
through the literature on pigs for a passage you could quote asserting
that they can't?  Likewise, if you want to look for neat quotable 
passages that address the question "Do chimps or wolves manifest
territoriality at the level of the individual", which was the question 
at issue, I think you will look long without finding anything.  That's 
why I left it up to you.  After all, the set of behaviors which a given 
species does manifest is limited, while the set of things which they do not
do is infinite.  Most authors therefore tend to concentrate on the first set.
   Discussing the anthropological question, it's easy to find quotes
that sum up one position or another, but for most such it's dubious
how much they will actually contribute.  Here, I'll give you one:
R. B. Lee, "Reflections on primitive communism", in Tim Ingold et. al.,
_Hunters and Gatherers 1:  History, Evolution, and Social Change_, p. 253:

	Before the rise of the state and the entrenchment of
	social inequality, people lived for millenia in small-scale 
	kin-based social groups, in which the core institutions of
	economic life included collective of common ownership of land
	and resources, generalized reciprocity in the distribution
	of food, and relatively egalitarian political relations.
	This basic pattern, with variations, has been observed
	in literally hundreds of non-state societies, as 
	indicated, for example, in Murdock's _Ethnographic
	Atlas_ (1967).

But now what have we gained?  Lee says they did; Benson, I take it,
says they didn't really.  So where are we?  I might argue, well,
Lee at least is an anthropologist, and the paper is in an 
anthropological publication, so he's more reliable, but that's
not a line of argument that I'd want to be too free with.  And
already from this quote, and more if you read the rest of his paper, 
you get a distinct impression that to him this way of living is an 
unqualified Good Thing, and that he has some hopes that humanity might
return to some postmodern version of it some day--and that tendentious note
impugns whatever extra credibility anyone might want to accord him on the
basis of his anthropological credentials.  (This quite independently of
whether you do or don't share his views).
    IMO if we are addressing arguments of the type "all human
societies manifest X, (so we can conclude Y)", the only useful
appeals to authority are references to specific ethnographic
data documenting counterexamples.  So I'm delighted to see the debate
beginning to be cast in terms like the following:

> I need find only one example to cast doubt on your ambitious
> claim that "there is no such thing as . . .".  Here it is:

> The Yurok, Hupa, and Karok tribes that occupied what is now Northern
> California subsisted on salmon and whatever else they could glean from the
> surrounding forests.  Yet they recognized individual rights to geographic
> territory--mostly fishing places.  They had a quite sophisticated system
> for establishing and adjudicating claims to such real property.  See Walter
> Goldsmidt, "Ethics and the Structure of Society:  An Ethnological
> Contribution to the Sociology of Knowledge," 53 AMERICAN ANTHROPOLOGIST 506

Well, we seem to be tossing the burden of proof back an forth--I think
because I'm trying to deal with four or five opponents at once, and
it's not clear that you're all making exactly the same claims, or even
addressing the same questions.  I entered the debate to refute a claim 
that land ownership is universal in humans and other species.  So as far as
I'm concerned I only need one counterexample.  I gave one in another post; 
there are scores more well-documented in the anthropological literature.

> As you so pungently put it yourself, Scott, 
>>It's a shame to let a few facts get in the way of a perfectly good
>>theory, eh?

Since I don't have a theory to push, I can't take this too seriously.

> Do you really believe, Scott, that ethology and anthropology are hard
> enough sciences to support your faith in "pure and simple" conclusions?  I
> can understand having an opinion about the strength of the various theories
> (hence my careful phrasing about my belief about available data), but your
> earnestness strikes me as either charmingly naive or sadly doctrinaire.  

Here once again I think we have a pernicious slippage between questions of
fact and questions of theory.  Ethology, and to a lesser but real extent
anthropology, are sufficiently empirical that we can arrive, by systematic
observation, at sets of objectively established facts.  For example, there
is a lot of room for debate about whether a particular Klamath story is
an astronomical code, an account of the origin of the incest taboo, a 
cautionary tale, an origin myth describing the cause of certain meteorological
patterns, or several or all of the above.  That is the realm of
anthropological theory.  There is no room for debate about whether they
told the story--they did.  That is the realm of ethnographic fact.
Likewise, there is room for interminable debate about the significance
of the fact that the northern Dene have no concept of individual land
ownership.  But there is no room for debate, and no debate involving
anyone who knows the data, about whether they aboriginally had such
a concept.  They didn't.  That too is the realm of fact.

> Though I think that the evidence I have cited deflates your overly-broad
> claim about the universality of real property rights, I also think that you
> have a valid point.  I should have read your intial claim more carefully;
> you were not attacking property rights per se, but only the idea that all
> types of social animals and human societies recognize individual claims to
> real property.  You could have supported this claim by giving merely *one*
> example of a human society that ignored real property.

Precisely so, and that was all I ever intended to do.
  
> You mentioned Eskimos as one such society.

I don't think I did, but I expect that they constitute another good
example.

> But I conclude that none of this seems to make any important difference in
> how we regard property rights.  The deep principle is that customary legal
> systems come to protect scarce resources.  Land is very, very often such a
> resource; especially in farming cultures (such as the Ifuago of Northern
> Luzon) but sometimes also in hunter-gatherer ones (such as the Yuroks). 
> Sometimes, however, there is so much land of so little value that a
> customary legal system ignores rights to it (see the Comanche).  

> These variations on a common theme tell us nothing about the validity of
> individual real property rights, though.  Finding a culture that recognized
> *no* individual property rights *would*.  I have never heard of such a
> culture, and thus stick to my working hypothesis that we have a
> deep--perhaps even inborn--desire to own property.  What specific kind of
> property we end up recognizing, will depend on what we need to survive and
> what we find hard to get.

There's a lot to be said here, but I don't think I disagree with much of
this.  The debate that I've been engaged in was concerned purely with
individual land ownership.


