Date: Mon, 22 Feb 93 10:49:42 CST
From: eder@hsvaic.boeing.com (Dani Eder)
Subject: police market

Here is my scenario of what would happen if police 'protection' were
less available.

(1) Stores would be designed to be more resistant to vandalism.

In the part of New York City where my parents live (central Bronx),
just about every store has a roll-down metal portcullis (I borrow
the term from castle design).  The more resistant ones are solid
strips of sheet metal about 6 inches high spanning the width of
the storefront.  Each strip is formed to interlock with the strips
above and below to form a solid shield when rolled down, but which
stores by rolling up at the top.  They can survive an auto impact
without tearing away.

These sorts of protection are uncommon where I now live, in north
Alabama, but very prevalent in NY City.

(2) Stores would adopt procedures to limit theft risk.

I used to buy silver from a jewelry store/fabricator (they made
jewelrey to order in a sop in the back room) in Washington State.
To get in the store, you had to be buzzed in by an employee.

There were always a couple of store employees (behind the counter,
not guards) who were visibly armed - i.e. a large pistol on a
gun belt.  There was a sign telling you that the store employees
have been trained how to use their guns - so don't even think
about trying to steal from them.  They did these things because
they had a lot of valuble stock (raw gold wire & bar, gems) in
addition to the usual finished jewelry.

(3) Additional security provisions

I would expect that private store use of metal detectors would
become more common, and adoption of the double-lock security I
observed at Lawrence Livermore National Lab.  There you went
into a first door, which electrically latched, then had to pass
a challenge (insert a card into a reader and key in a secret
code), then you would get buzzed in through the second door.
It functioned as an air-lock.  They actually had a scale under
you to weigh you, so that someone else couldn't use your badge
(I was there for one day visit, and they weighed me at the
visitor center at the same time I selected my 4-digit code).

A civilian adaption might have a freely-open outside door,
a video screen and camera to communicate with the store staff,
and a buzzed inner door.  There could be a civilian metal
detector built into the 'airlock' to screen for guns.

All of this security stuff might run a couple of thousand dollars,
but the stores might recoup some of that in lower theft 
insurance premiums, and more from lower property taxes that
went to pay for police.

